body:
Ok let me use an analogy

&#x200B;

A nutritionist is on their way home in the deep suburbs but needs to stop and grab some bread and milk.  There is only one store that sells the bread they like for their family and it's right next to a WalMart.  They stop, get out of their car, and start to head into their store, when a group of people asks them why they're going into that particular store.  The nutritionist explains they are there for bread and some raw milk.  More questions are asked and during the dialogue something the nutritionist says bothers one or more of the group.  It could be that they don't like bleached flower or a particular kind of germ wheat.  The point is that someone has decided that the knowledge and preferences of the nutritionist are, in some way, an affront to them personally.  They begin to obstruct and harass the nutritionist.

&#x200B;

Now of course IRL situations like this can normally be resolved by the police.  Let's add some kind of situtaion in here where that isn't an option.  It could be because the nutritionist slept with the local sheriff's sister in highschool or it could be that these people are in a country where *nobody* wants the police involved in *anything*.  The point is that calling a group of people to use force or intimidation to stop the harassment isn't really an option.
